<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Beihan</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1967 CE (approx.) · capital Suq Abdullah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A frontier emirate in Wadi Bayhan, ancient heart of Qataban, whose sharifs were staunch British allies against Yemen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-bayhan">Wadi Bayhan</a>, once the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-qataban">Kingdom of Qataban</a>, was by the 19th century divided between local tribes and a family of sharifs, descendants of the Prophet, who emerged as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> around 1800 with their seat at Suq Abdullah.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Beihan lay on the disputed border between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>' <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a>, and clashes there were frequent in the 1950s. Its ruling sharif, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ahmad-al-habili">Husayn ibn Ahmad al-Habili</a>, was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain's</a> firmest allies and a founding figure of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south">Federation of Arab Emirates of the South</a> in 1959. Agriculture depended on seasonal floods.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> took over the region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was incorporated into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>; its ruling family went into exile.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-beihan.jpg" alt="Emirate of Beihan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Beihan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1967 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suq Abdullah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Husayn ibn Ahmad al-Habili</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Beihan, Bayhan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=emirate-of-beihan" aria-label="Emirate of Beihan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5932 1678 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Beihan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6252 1934L6295 1853M6252 1934L6313 1863M6252 1934L6313 1863M6252 1934L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6295 1853h0M6313 1863h0M6313 1863h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6252 1934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6252 1934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=emirate-of-beihan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
